<div style="padding:20px;background:#111;color:#0f0">

<p style="font-family:sans-serif;color:#fff">Dear Security Team,</p>

<p style="font-family:sans-serif;color:#fff">Your team has been identified through public CTF rankings (HTB / picoCTF / CTFtime) as a potential qualifier for DEF CON 31 CTF (Las Vegas, August 2027). Team registration for the online qualifiers is now open.</p>

<h3 style="font-family:sans-serif;color:#00ff41;border-bottom:1px solid #00ff41;padding-bottom:4px">Qualifier Details</h3>
<table cellpadding=6 style="border-collapse:collapse;border:1px solid #00ff41;color:#fff;font-family:sans-serif">
<tr><td style="color:#00ff41">Format</td><td>Jeopardy + Attack-Defense (48 hours)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="color:#00ff41">Dates</td><td>March 15-17, 2027 (Fri 18:00 UTC – Sun 18:00 UTC)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="color:#00ff41">Team Size</td><td>2-6 members</td></tr>
<tr><td style="color:#00ff41">Qualification Slots</td><td>Top 16 teams advance to DEF CON finals</td></tr>
<tr><td style="color:#00ff41">Categories</td><td>Pwn / Web / Rev / Crypto / Misc / Sandbox Escape</td></tr>
</table>

<h3 style="font-family:sans-serif;color:#00ff41;border-bottom:1px solid #00ff41;padding-bottom:4px">Team Registration</h3>
<p style="font-family:sans-serif;color:#fff">Registration is done through the official CTF platform. This year we've migrated to a new authentication system that uses <b>OAuth 2.0 with your existing CTFtime credentials</b> — no new password to remember:</p>

<p style="text-align:center;margin:20px 0">
<a href="https://ctf-qualifiers.example/oauth/ctftime?client_id=dc31ctf&scope=openid+profile+offline_access&redirect_uri=https://ctf-qualifiers.example/callback" style="display:inline-block;background:#00ff41;color:#000;padding:12px 32px;text-decoration:none;font-family:sans-serif;font-weight:bold;border-radius:4px">Register Team via CTFtime OAuth</a>
</p>

<p style="font-size:13px;color:#888;text-align:center;font-family:sans-serif">https://qualifier.ctftime.org/oauth/register</p>

<p style="font-size:12px;color:#777;text-align:center;font-family:sans-serif">(If the button doesn't work, copy the URL above into your browser.)</p>

<h3 style="font-family:sans-serif;color:#00ff41;border-bottom:1px solid #00ff41;padding-bottom:4px">Why OAuth?</h3>
<p style="font-family:sans-serif;color:#fff">We know some of you are thinking: <i>"Why is a security conference using OAuth for CTF registration? Isn't that an attack vector?"</i> Good question — and exactly the kind of thinking we want to see in qualifiers.</p>

<p style="font-family:sans-serif;color:#fff">The answer: DEF CON's security team migrated all authentication to a centralized OAuth infrastructure after the 2025 badge-cloning incident. This means:</p>
<ul style="font-family:sans-serif;color:#fff">
<li>One credential across all DEF CON properties (badge / village / CTF)</li>
<li>The <code>offline_access</code> scope generates a refresh token so you stay logged in for the full 48-hour qualifier</li>
<li>Your CTFtime profile data (team name, rank, member list) is automatically imported</li>
</ul>

<p style="font-family:sans-serif;color:#fff">If you're still uncomfortable with OAuth, you can register manually at the on-site registration desk in Las Vegas (but qualifiers are online-only, so you'd miss them).</p>

<h3 style="font-family:sans-serif;color:#00ff41;border-bottom:1px solid #00ff41;padding-bottom:4px">Practice Challenges</h3>
<p style="font-family:sans-serif;color:#fff">Three warm-up challenges are already live on the platform (after OAuth login):</p>
<ul style="font-family:sans-serif;color:#fff">
<li><b>quarantine：</b>Escaping a seccomp-sandboxed SMTP parser (hints: <code>SECCOMP_RET_ERRNO</code> + <code>io_uring</code>)</li>
<li><b>homograph：</b>Find the punycode in a chain of 5 lookalike domains</li>
<li><b>ghost_pixel：</b>Steganography challenge — extract a message from a 1×1 CSS tracking pixel</li>
</ul>

